#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096323 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29956ecb-a28a-5406-b8dc-9776e9a3ccee" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Polynomial Raised to a Power Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19bf8d06-cbcc-547b-b882-ac89cd39fd1a" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-181" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = (3x^4+x^3)^5$, calculate $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x}$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf = g^5, \\qquad g = 3x^4+x^3\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g} = 5g^4\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x} = 12x^3 + 3x^2\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{df}{dx} &= 5g^{4} \\cdot (12x^{3} + 3x^{2}) \\\\\n&= 5(3x^{4} + x^{3})^{4}(12x^{3} + 3x^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096324 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f0cdac9-57b2-5634-8d98-d6adf8a85204" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Radical Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8fcdb2c8-71ca-5bfa-b7fe-49f8f2c05bbb" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-182" :question/problem "Given that $y = \\sqrt{1-x^2}$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nHere, we've used $u$ instead of $g$ for the intermediate variable.\n\nLet $u=1-x^2$. Then\n\n$$\ny = \\sqrt u = u^{1/2}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\dfrac{1}{2}u^{-1/2} = \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt u}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -2x\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}} \\cdot (-2x) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2x}{2\\sqrt{u}} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1 - x^{2}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096325 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a312840-0d35-50bb-94b3-b04778f4efc3" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Using the Chain Rule and Prime Notation"}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c37ae73-233d-5e0d-87ff-05cacc6d078b" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-183" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-3x}}$, calculate $f'(x)$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Write $f(x)=(1-3x)^{-1/2}$. Let $u=1-3x$, so $du/dx=-3$. By the chain rule,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf'(x)&=-\\frac12u^{-3/2}\\frac{du}{dx}\\\\\n&=-\\frac12u^{-3/2}(-3)\\\\\n&=\\frac{3}{2(1-3x)^{3/2}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe original function and this derivative are defined for $x<1/3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244537 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f0cdac9-57b2-5634-8d98-d6adf8a85204" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Radical Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f261db81-a459-51b2-a6ff-6738ddf27ce0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3-6x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244539} {:answer/id #uuid "b49f1c47-c5b2-5898-a141-252c0dd9f49f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{3-6x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244540} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba7fb2e-9930-584f-936d-8d9299d1fcca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12\\sqrt{3-6x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244541} {:answer/id #uuid "f322aba6-1fbc-5c83-98ec-7a682b518644" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3-6x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244542} {:answer/id #uuid "0bb46c65-569c-5130-b09b-f4d548d0108e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3-6x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f261db81-a459-51b2-a6ff-6738ddf27ce0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3-6x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244539} :answer-field/id #uuid "da1e4f69-116a-51a5-b38d-526b338808f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244538}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6fbc1a64-7e7b-5925-877b-d842ceed3e27" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-107291" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\sqrt{3-6x},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=3-6x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}={u}^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-6.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3-6x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244544 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a312840-0d35-50bb-94b3-b04778f4efc3" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Using the Chain Rule and Prime Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f484a0b2-190f-5094-b024-ad92ae95b880" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(3x+4{)}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244546} {:answer/id #uuid "9bd0b909-f5e9-57ec-a1e0-41726e67c597" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(3x+4{)}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244547} {:answer/id #uuid "a867e9ef-beee-5973-9813-7a556d335def" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15(3x+4{)}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244548} {:answer/id #uuid "62106752-6762-5b03-8fd1-71ed4473a29f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9(3x+4{)}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244549} {:answer/id #uuid "b08e8d94-e9a8-50af-b6f8-bbd50b7a2e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20(3x+4{)}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a867e9ef-beee-5973-9813-7a556d335def" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15(3x+4{)}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244548} :answer-field/id #uuid "527b1a99-a410-5b68-a1ba-52b5a12ff2ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244545}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "671e307e-4df5-50bb-8e5c-121d921a324b" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-107293" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(3x+4)}^{5},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=3x+4$ and $f(g(x))=g(x{)}^{5}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{5}{)}^{′}=5{g}^{4}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(3x+4{)}^{′}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =5{g}^{4}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =15(3x+4{)}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29956ecb-a28a-5406-b8dc-9776e9a3ccee" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Polynomial Raised to a Power Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dde46f62-0e8a-5937-8599-447986204348" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30{(5x+10)}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244553} {:answer/id #uuid "dca110dc-5ab7-5744-b84c-2257e5ed49f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{(5x+10)}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244554} {:answer/id #uuid "c1fd9c23-4b19-53fa-9aeb-7fc689ebf7f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10{(5x+10)}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244555} {:answer/id #uuid "4a8f7e36-1dc3-5309-a62b-70d51a7a99f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(5x+10)}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244556} {:answer/id #uuid "592db5fa-308d-51ae-8dad-09a44868c0ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30{(5x+10)}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dde46f62-0e8a-5937-8599-447986204348" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30{(5x+10)}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244553} :answer-field/id #uuid "f854dc8c-7fd9-57d7-8b3b-7fc5bbf2526b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244552}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a2593795-1dac-5179-a216-eae1eb3b01c5" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-11410" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)={(5x+10)}^{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{6},\\,g=5x+10\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=6{g}^{5},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=5.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =6{g}^{5}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =30(5x+10{)}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f0cdac9-57b2-5634-8d98-d6adf8a85204" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Radical Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c76e134d-97f0-52ae-81e0-08ec088bdec9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2x+7)^3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244560} {:answer/id #uuid "fa95c1d6-3469-51b6-ac2b-ba7ab164518b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2x+7)^3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa95c1d6-3469-51b6-ac2b-ba7ab164518b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2x+7)^3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244561} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d72aba1-a4a8-5bcd-9a5c-71653263fc1c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244559}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "01d86967-f170-52eb-884c-faade3aec8eb" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-314194" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+7}}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2x+7.$ Then $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}={u}^{-1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2x+7{)}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244562 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29956ecb-a28a-5406-b8dc-9776e9a3ccee" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Polynomial Raised to a Power Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "331946e1-ff14-5810-9cfc-9f1687ca1f2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "331946e1-ff14-5810-9cfc-9f1687ca1f2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244564} :answer-field/id #uuid "25e736c3-94dc-5701-b9f3-7197af2f78b0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244563}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6c621db2-dac4-5875-9a39-cffdb06bb23a" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-342630" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={({x}^{2}+2x+1)}^{2},$ the slope at the point $(0,1)$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nFirst, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{2},\\,g={x}^{2}+2x+1\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=2g,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=2x+2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =2g\\cdot (2x+2) \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+2x+1)(2x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line at the point where $x=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & =[2({x}^{2}+2x+1)(2x+2){]}_{x=0} \\\\  & =2((0{)}^{2}+2\\cdot (0)+1)(2\\cdot (0)+2) \\\\  & =2(1)(2) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244565 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29956ecb-a28a-5406-b8dc-9776e9a3ccee" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Polynomial Raised to a Power Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aee374a3-f35d-5a18-9168-bbd221cd532b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244567} {:answer/id #uuid "e9d6eb93-abe6-506e-9f4d-27d032f5b307" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aee374a3-f35d-5a18-9168-bbd221cd532b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244567} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e0a6ce5-d38e-5ba3-9859-5d1d4c8be904" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244566}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b8f5684d-fcfe-57b2-9c61-e436cca4178c" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-342663" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(3x+1)}^{4},$ the slope at the point $(-1,16)$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use chain rule to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nFirst, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{4},\\,g=3x+1\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=4{g}^{3},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =4{g}^{3}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12(3x+1{)}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line at the point where $x=-1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-1} & =[12(3x+1{)}^{3}{]}_{x=-1} \\\\  & =12(3\\cdot (-1)+1{)}^{3} \\\\  & =12(-2{)}^{3} \\\\  & =-96.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244569 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f0cdac9-57b2-5634-8d98-d6adf8a85204" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Radical Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5182c3ce-69e9-54b7-8ca0-bdfb0cf46ea8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3x+6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5182c3ce-69e9-54b7-8ca0-bdfb0cf46ea8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3x+6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244571} :answer-field/id #uuid "94580e65-33b1-57e3-b860-a15cceb7db2a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244570}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d81a556c-ff67-59a3-a319-5cc4ee47cadf" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-342683" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\sqrt{3x+6}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=3x+6.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3x+6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f0cdac9-57b2-5634-8d98-d6adf8a85204" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Radical Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e72d69d4-a8d1-5761-8459-d98d546bccdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244574} {:answer/id #uuid "2bdc1950-359f-5541-b420-602c2a526809" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2bdc1950-359f-5541-b420-602c2a526809" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244575} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfae2bb8-1a73-532a-855c-add69030b974" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244573}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8e4c4d02-93ce-585f-897c-bbbbeb8472bc" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-342802" :question/problem "The slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}+12x}}$ at the point where $x=2$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=3{x}^{2}+12x.$ Then $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}={u}^{-1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6x+12.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\cdot (6x+12) \\\\  & =-\\frac{6x+12}{2\\sqrt{(3{x}^{2}+12x{)}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=2} & ={(-\\frac{6x+12}{2\\sqrt{(3{x}^{2}+12x{)}^{3}}})|}_{x=2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6(2)+12}{2\\sqrt{(3(2{)}^{2}+12(2){)}^{3}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{24}{2\\sqrt{{36}^{3}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244576 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a312840-0d35-50bb-94b3-b04778f4efc3" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Using the Chain Rule and Prime Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "446e1606-6e23-59df-809c-3dad86a219c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20(4x+3)^4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "446e1606-6e23-59df-809c-3dad86a219c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20(4x+3)^4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244578} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d686353-1af2-55f8-88ee-5ce6c72e35b1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244577}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3f885ec7-a45a-50f8-bd02-b98b6beb52a3" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-342804" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(4x+3)}^{5},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=4x+3$ and $f(g(x))=g(x{)}^{5}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{5}{)}^{′}=5{g}^{4}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(4x+3{)}^{′}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =5{g}^{4}\\cdot (4) \\\\  & =20(4x+3{)}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244579 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a312840-0d35-50bb-94b3-b04778f4efc3" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Using the Chain Rule and Prime Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c186c42-6d6d-58f3-89cf-f49d78134d11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4x^3+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c186c42-6d6d-58f3-89cf-f49d78134d11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4x^3+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244581} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b3085ea-cb96-51c3-ab50-005850c14858" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244580}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "461f2109-94b8-5a39-93db-8a3706492d5a" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-342891" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}+5}},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}+5{)}^{3}}}($\n{{field-1}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=2{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}+5$ and $f(g(x))=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{g(x)}}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & ={(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{g}})}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{g}^{3}}}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(2{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}+5{)}^{′}=8{x}^{3}-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{g}^{3}}}\\cdot (8{x}^{3}-2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}+5{)}^{3}}}(\\,x-4{x}^{3}\\,).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29956ecb-a28a-5406-b8dc-9776e9a3ccee" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Polynomial Raised to a Power Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4009a7a8-f560-5d9d-85b4-69dc9ec95449" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6x+11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4009a7a8-f560-5d9d-85b4-69dc9ec95449" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6x+11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244584} :answer-field/id #uuid "810de85e-ab03-5b53-8538-bb4a0f108607" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244583}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8d4112c3-c428-58ac-aa36-5e59e60a5e82" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-342910" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=(3-2x{)}^{3},$ the equation of the tangent at the point $(2,-1)$ is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nFirst, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{3},\\,g=3-2x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=3{g}^{2},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =3{g}^{2}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-6(3-2x{)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line at the point where $x=2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=2} & =(-6{(3-2x)}^{2}){|}_{x=2} \\\\  & =-6{(3-2(2))}^{2} \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line at the point $(2,-1)$ is $m=-6.$ Now, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+1 & =-6\\cdot (x-2) \\\\ y & =-6x+12-1 \\\\ y & =-6x+11\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097171 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f0cdac9-57b2-5634-8d98-d6adf8a85204" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Radical Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c720d59-e194-5266-b56a-c466a517a985" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5x - 1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095384} {:answer/id #uuid "f0193492-5011-5859-a30f-bbc8de469e22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{5x - 1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095385} {:answer/id #uuid "0fb017af-74fd-54ab-b3e7-3cf1930e878c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5x - 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095386} {:answer/id #uuid "144cb047-1208-5ac8-84b5-438b8948026c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac1{2\\sqrt{(5x-1)^3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095387} {:answer/id #uuid "628b0cb7-1d47-5296-86cf-7c96168acd76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac5{2\\sqrt{(5x-1)^3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "628b0cb7-1d47-5296-86cf-7c96168acd76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac5{2\\sqrt{(5x-1)^3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095388} :answer-field/id #uuid "8efc3cd7-9913-56b8-a464-cc38a8cdd7e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095383}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "854ec833-4bc0-54f0-9210-c708a9342fef" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-48730" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ for $y = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5x - 1}}$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Write $y=(5x-1)^{-1/2}$. For $x>1/5$, the chain rule gives\n$$y'=-\\frac12(5x-1)^{-3/2}\\cdot5=-\\frac5{2(5x-1)^{3/2}}.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29956ecb-a28a-5406-b8dc-9776e9a3ccee" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Polynomial Raised to a Power Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93d3a4bb-9046-5724-81a5-d6c8132b738b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(4+6x)^2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095828} {:answer/id #uuid "da4b4e40-db69-587e-bc11-6cc7a4c06e6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30(4 + 6x)^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095829} {:answer/id #uuid "dfd52c05-d5cf-599c-9ad4-6f7e03723c6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18x(4 + 6x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095830} {:answer/id #uuid "6a2fd34d-bfa7-516f-99c6-8073eadb8769" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(4 + 6x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095831} {:answer/id #uuid "7dcfc72a-1932-5f07-a4d3-625ca2e743dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18(4 + 6x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7dcfc72a-1932-5f07-a4d3-625ca2e743dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18(4 + 6x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095832} :answer-field/id #uuid "42bed18e-2ea3-5815-9102-05a6b242e840" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095827}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0bd56a8-5c99-5166-be8e-4185bfb62ed5" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-666" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{df}{dx}$ when $f(x) = (4 + 6x)^{3}$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The outer cube has derivative $3u^2$, and the inner function $u=4+6x$ has derivative $6$. Thus\n$$f'=3(4+6x)^2\\cdot6=18(4+6x)^2.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244585 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a312840-0d35-50bb-94b3-b04778f4efc3" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Using the Chain Rule and Prime Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd949ee5-144e-588e-9d34-2ff971252cf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8(8x-7{)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244587} {:answer/id #uuid "550a6d11-034c-5cfd-add0-01f820f9901a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16(8x-7{)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244588} {:answer/id #uuid "85430447-56e2-54be-955a-609b9ff1b048" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16(16x-7{)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244589} {:answer/id #uuid "3146bcab-2018-55b5-884a-0e6adab784e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32(16x-7{)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244590} {:answer/id #uuid "cbf70b7c-4713-5c27-a784-b7cb739a3cfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32(8x-7{)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbf70b7c-4713-5c27-a784-b7cb739a3cfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32(8x-7{)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244591} :answer-field/id #uuid "07524d9f-33c6-5c8b-84a5-3165cb41df11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244586}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d86f99aa-86d1-5875-846a-ef04f0925294" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-71954" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(8x-7)}^{4},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=8x-7$ and $f(g(x))=g(x{)}^{4}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{4}{)}^{′}=4{g}^{3}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(8x-7{)}^{′}=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =4{g}^{3}\\cdot (8) \\\\  & =32(8x-7{)}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244592 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a312840-0d35-50bb-94b3-b04778f4efc3" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Using the Chain Rule and Prime Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04bce5ff-030a-51e2-a2f9-4e96ac1cd564" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15(2-5x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244594} {:answer/id #uuid "5e5d534f-9d61-5da6-a247-5425ad14f9d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15(2-5x{)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244595} {:answer/id #uuid "8abf7c00-e618-5e47-b7ca-e3fb931c02f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15(2-5x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244596} {:answer/id #uuid "9f614676-3109-5eeb-8a0a-ebd7282b49c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15(2+5x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244597} {:answer/id #uuid "0df93ae9-22cc-55a7-8da8-9ba8f076fdc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15(2+5x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8abf7c00-e618-5e47-b7ca-e3fb931c02f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15(2-5x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244596} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3992b64-0510-5227-bfa6-4e9505929bf6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244593}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f357ccca-d377-5d12-8cff-fa5dd0c65cd6" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-71956" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(2-5x)}^{3},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=2-5x$ and $f(g(x))=g(x{)}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{3}{)}^{′}=3{g}^{2}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(2-5x{)}^{′}=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =3{g}^{2}\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-15(2-5x{)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
